after a computer restart, chrome can restore all the previously opened tabs and windows. When that happens I want the resync to find the previously opened tabs that correspond to the tabs that are now opened in Chrome. I dont want to lose the old tab's placement in the tree so new windows tabs might need to be used in place of the older tabs. since a restore can happen after initialization, when new tabs are added, we may need to see if the new tabs/windows are already present and roughly in the right place. I want best effort, but err on the side of duplication. 
I prefer SQL over JS, so the backend loops I would like to replace with SQL statements. Batch operations with RETURNING clauses should be the norm. where multiple statements are excuted in sequence, put them in the same sqlExec call. I prefer dense code that is terse. SQL helps with that. If a snipet of code is complicated enough to warrent a comment, keep all comments for the function together to explain the high level all at once.
I also prefer a functional style over imperative, so when there is an oppertunity to refactor something to be more functional, please do so.
once a tab is saved, it sould stay saved unless manually closed. because of this, the `save on close` action rule should trigger whenever a tab is added or refreshed and just set the saved status at that time. The frontend should have a way to display that a tab is saved, probably with a default green circle prepending the node, like the defauld red circle for duplicates. dont forget to add the color to the configuration. I think this might be working, but I want it doublechecked and refactored to be SQL driven.
the `move to window` action should move to a named window using the node_text. If such a window does not exist, one must be made. Once made, the window node should be saved, but not the chilren (at least not by default). 
The delete action rule should also close the tab. verify this while you are refactoring.
the action config layout isn't lined up very well, can you make it look prettier while maintaining the general aesthetic.
While you refactor the codebase, try to find any bugs or inconsistentcies. In particular, I am worried about the node structure. We have 2 competing trees that need to be kept in sync: the chrome view of active tabs/windows/groups, and the TabSQL view of active and saved tabs/windows/groups. for example, when a node in the TabSQL view is moved, it and all its decendents must be moved in the chrome state (ie, the referer relatoinships should be maintained as much as possible). If a node is moved in the Chrome view, its descendence should not be moved and instead reassigned to the moved node's parent (their order_by would also need to be updated - I also want order_by maintained at each level, that is, WHERE parent_id is the same). 
